---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-gas-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Electric Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-electric-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best 50 Gallon Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-50-gallon-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Power Vent Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-power-vent-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Types of Water Heaters",
    "url": "/types-of-water-heaters/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Water Heater Replacement Cost",
    "url": "/water-heater-replacement-cost/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "7 Best 40 Gallon Water Heaters in 2026: Tall, Short, Lowboy and Mobile Home Picks";
const description =
  "The best 40 gallon water heaters for one to three people: tall, short and lowboy gas and electric tanks for closets and mobile homes, compared on first-hour rating.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ge-gg40t08bxr",
    "name": "GE RealMAX Choice GG40T08BXR 40-Gallon Tall Natural Gas",
    "category": "Natural Gas, Atmospheric Vent, 40,000 BTU, Tall",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed at an 83-gallon first hour, more than many 50-gallon electric tanks",
      "40,000 BTU burner on a 40-gallon tank recovers quickly",
      "Eight-year limited tank and parts warranty, longer than the six years most 40s carry",
      "Narrow 20-inch diameter suits tight corners"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tall at about 61-7/8 inches, so it will not go under a low shelf",
      "Atmospheric vent needs an existing chimney or B-vent"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The 40-gallon tank to buy for a house with gas and a flue: 83 gallons in the first hour and eight years of warranty, in a 20-inch footprint.",
    "description": "The GG40T08BXR is the reason a 40-gallon gas tank can be enough for three people. GE lists it at an 83-gallon first-hour rating, which is more hot water in the first hour than a standard 4,500W 50-gallon electric tank delivers, because its 40,000 BTU burner is large for a 40-gallon tank. That is the same burner size many 50-gallon atmospheric tanks use, so recovery is fast and a household of two or three is unlikely to run out. GE's spec table lists a 0.64 UEF, a typical figure for an atmospheric gas tank. The warranty is the other standout: eight years on the tank and parts, against six on most of the competition at this size. It is a tall model, about 61-7/8 inches high on retailer listings and 20 inches across, so it fits narrow spaces well but needs headroom; listings put the weight at around 140 to 147 pounds. Atmospheric venting means no fan and no outlet, but the house needs a working chimney or B-vent, and the tank must have combustion air. For the most common 40-gallon replacement, a gas tank in a basement or garage, this is our first choice."
  },
  {
    "id": "ge-ge40s08dat",
    "name": "GE GE40S08DAT 40-Gallon Short Electric",
    "category": "Electric, 4,500W Dual Element, Short",
    "badge": "Best Electric 40 Gallon",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listing gives a 63-gallon first-hour rating, unusually high for a 40-gallon electric",
      "Short height of about 52-7/8 inches on GE's product page",
      "Eight-year warranty listed by retailers",
      "Standard 240V, 4,500W elements on an ordinary 30-amp circuit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "22 inches wide, two inches wider than a tall 40",
      "Electric recovery is slow once the stored water is used"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The electric 40 to buy for a closet or a basement with low ducts: short, a 63-gallon first hour and an eight-year warranty.",
    "description": "The GE40S08DAT is the short 40-gallon electric tank we would put in a closet or under basement ductwork. GE's product page lists it at 52-7/8 inches tall and 22 inches wide, and the listing gives a 63-gallon first-hour rating, which is high for a 40-gallon electric tank and close to many standard 50-gallon electrics. It uses two 4,500W elements at 240V, so it runs on the usual 30-amp double-pole circuit and drops in where an older 40-gallon electric tank was. Retailers list an eight-year warranty, longer than the six years most competing 40-gallon electrics carry. GE pitches it at two to four person households; we would say two to three, because once the stored water is used, a 4,500W element reheats only around 20 gallons an hour. The short design is the real reason to choose it over a tall tank: it clears a shelf in a utility closet and fits under the lower ducts and beams of older basements, where a 60-inch tank would not. If your space is tight in width rather than height, look at the tall Bradford White below instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-gidds-2487245",
    "name": "Rheem Professional Classic 40-Gallon Short Natural Gas (PROG40S-34N RH62, GIDDS-2487245)",
    "category": "Natural Gas, Atmospheric Vent, 34,000 BTU, Short",
    "badge": "Best Short Gas",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Short gas tank, listed by Rheem at about 52 inches tall",
      "34,000 BTU atmospheric burner, no fan or outlet needed",
      "Six-year limited tank and parts warranty on Rheem's product page",
      "Rheem Professional Classic parts are widely stocked by plumbers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Smaller burner than the GE, so slower recovery",
      "21 inches wide, wider than a tall 40"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The gas 40 for low basements and closets with a flue: short enough to fit under ducts, with Rheem's parts support behind it.",
    "description": "Not every gas water heater location has 5 feet of clear height. This short Rheem Professional Classic, model PROG40S-34N RH62 (sold on Amazon under the supply code GIDDS-2487245), is the gas 40 for basements with low ductwork, closets with a shelf, and spaces under stairs. Rheem's product page lists it at about 52 inches tall and 21 inches wide, roughly 10 inches shorter than the tall GE above, and it uses a 34,000 BTU atmospheric burner with no fan or outlet. The smaller burner means slower recovery than the GE's 40,000 BTU, so it suits a household of one or two people, or three with staggered showers. We could not confirm a first-hour figure for this exact model and have left it out. Rheem's page gives a six-year limited tank and parts warranty. Short gas tanks still need the vent connector to rise from the draft hood, and they still need combustion air, so a closet installation must meet those requirements. Choose it when height is the limit; choose the GE when it is not."
  },
  {
    "id": "bradford-white-re340t6-1ncww",
    "name": "Bradford White RE340T6-1NCWW 40-Gallon Tall Electric",
    "category": "Electric, 4,500W Dual Element, Tall",
    "badge": "Narrowest Electric",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Tall and narrow, at about 20 inches in diameter on distributor listings",
      "Hydrojet sediment-reduction inlet",
      "Six-year limited tank and component warranty on distributor listings",
      "Brand widely serviced by professional plumbers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Distributors list a first-hour rating of 49 to 53 gallons, lower than the GE short",
      "Mainly sold through wholesalers, so Amazon availability varies"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The electric 40 for spaces that are tight in width: 20 inches across, from the brand plumbers like to service.",
    "description": "The RE340T6 is Bradford White's tall 40-gallon electric tank, and its strength is footprint. At about 20 inches across and roughly 60 to 61 inches tall on distributor listings, it is narrower than the GE short electric and fits between a furnace and a wall or in a narrow closet with full height. Distributors list a first-hour rating of 49 to 53 gallons depending on the version, which is lower than the GE short, so it is a tank for one or two people, or three with careful timing. The Hydrojet inlet spins incoming cold water to reduce sediment build-up on the lower element, which matters most in hard-water areas. Distributor listings give a six-year limited warranty on the tank and components. Bradford White sells through plumbing wholesalers rather than big-box stores, so Amazon pricing and stock can move around, and some installers will only fit a tank they supplied. One distributor notes that units built before December 2022 are rated at 40 gallons, and some current listings describe this model as 39 gallons. Confirm the label before you compare it with other tanks."
  },
  {
    "id": "ao-smith-gcr-40lp",
    "name": "A.O. Smith GCR-40LP ProMax Plus 40-Gallon Liquid Propane",
    "category": "Liquid Propane, Atmospheric Vent, 36,000 BTU, Tall",
    "badge": "Best Propane",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built for propane from the factory, no conversion kit needed",
      "36,000 BTU burner on distributor listings",
      "Atmospheric vent: no fan or outlet, keeps working in a power cut",
      "A.O. Smith parts are widely stocked"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Distributors list about 38 gallons of actual storage",
      "We could not confirm the warranty term for this exact model"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The tank for a rural home on propane with a flue: factory-built for LP, with no fan to fail when the power goes out.",
    "description": "Homes without a natural gas main often run on propane, and that changes the choice. A tank built for natural gas cannot burn propane without a conversion, and most residential tank water heaters are not designed to be converted in the field. The GCR-40LP is A.O. Smith's 40-gallon tank built for liquid propane from the factory, with a 36,000 BTU burner listed by distributors. Supply houses list it at about 38 gallons of actual storage and a 0.62 UEF, typical of atmospheric gas tanks; a nominal 40-gallon rating with slightly less actual storage is common across the industry. Like the other atmospheric tanks here, it has no blower and no outlet, so it keeps heating during a power cut, which matters on a rural property where outages last longer. It needs a chimney or B-vent and combustion air. We could not confirm the current warranty term for this exact model number, so check it on the box or with A.O. Smith before buying. Propane costs more per unit of heat than natural gas, as our running-cost section shows, so this is the right tank only if propane is already your fuel."
  },
  {
    "id": "reliance-6-40-mdv",
    "name": "Reliance 6-40-MDV400 40-Gallon Mobile Home Direct Vent Gas",
    "category": "NG/LP Convertible, Direct Vent, 32,000 BTU, Manufactured Homes",
    "badge": "Best for Mobile Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed for manufactured home use, which a standard tank is not",
      "Direct vent draws combustion air from outside, sealed from the living space",
      "Convertible between natural gas and propane",
      "Six-year limited tank and parts warranty on the manufacturer's sheet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Vent kit is not included and must be bought separately",
      "32,000 BTU burner and 33 gallons per hour of recovery are modest"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The gas 40 for a mobile or manufactured home. A standard tank is the wrong product; this one is built and listed for the job.",
    "description": "A mobile or manufactured home needs a water heater listed for that use, and the Reliance 6 40 MDV is one of the few gas 40-gallon tanks sold on Amazon that is. Reliance is an A.O. Smith brand. It is a direct vent design: combustion air comes in from outside and exhaust goes out through the same sealed vent, so the burner never draws air from the living space. The manufacturer's sheet lists a 32,000 BTU input, 33 gallons per hour of recovery at a 90-degree rise, a 0.59 UEF and a 3-inch vent. It measures about 20 inches across and 58-1/4 inches tall, with side inlet and top outlet connections to match common manufactured-home layouts. The gas control converts between natural gas and propane, which suits parks and rural lots on either fuel. Reliance lists a six-year limited tank and parts warranty and one year of labor. The vent kit is sold separately, so price it with the tank. Recovery is modest, so this is a tank for one to three people. Our section on mobile homes below explains why a standard residential tank should not go in instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "ge-ge40l08dat",
    "name": "GE GE40L08DAT 36-Gallon Top Port Lowboy Electric",
    "category": "Electric, 4,500W Dual Element, Lowboy",
    "badge": "Best Lowboy",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "About 32-3/4 inches tall, low enough for under-counter and crawl spaces",
      "Listing gives a 53-gallon first-hour rating",
      "Top ports simplify connections in tight spaces",
      "Eight-year parts warranty on retailer listings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "36 gallons, not 40, which is typical of lowboy tanks",
      "27 inches wide, the widest tank on this page"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The tank for spaces with less than 3 feet of height. Lowboys trade 4 gallons and some width for a tank barely knee-high.",
    "description": "A lowboy tank exists for one reason: height. The GE40L08DAT is listed at about 32-3/4 inches tall, low enough to fit under a counter, in a crawl space, or in a low attic space where nothing taller will go. To get there it gives up width and a little capacity: retailers list it at 27 inches across, and it holds 36 gallons rather than 40, which is standard across most so-called 40-gallon lowboys. The listing gives a 53-gallon first-hour rating from two 4,500W elements at 240V, so it suits one or two people, or a small household with modest demand. The top ports put both water connections and the relief valve on top, which simplifies piping in a low enclosure. Retailers list an eight-year parts warranty, with one year of labor. If the tank sits in a crawl space or attic, it needs a drain pan, a relief valve line to a safe location, and access for service; a lowboy that cannot be reached cannot have its anode or elements replaced. Buy it only when height rules out every other tank here."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best 40 Gallon Water Heater",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-40-gallon-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many people does a 40 gallon water heater serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 40-gallon tank usually serves one to three people. On gas, with a burner around 40,000 BTU, it can serve three comfortably, because first-hour ratings reach the low 80s. On electric, first-hour ratings run about 50 to 63 gallons, so it suits two people comfortably and three with staggered showers. Four or more people should usually buy 50 gallons."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 40 gallon water heater big enough for a family of four?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not, especially on electric. A family of four typically uses 55 to 70 gallons in its busiest hour. A gas 40 with a large burner can sometimes keep up if showers are spread out, but most families of four are better served by a 50-gallon gas tank or a 50-gallon electric with high-recovery elements."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How tall is a 40 gallon water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Tall 40-gallon tanks are about 58 to 62 inches high and around 20 inches across. Short versions are around 52 to 53 inches high and 21 to 22 inches across. Lowboys, which usually hold 36 gallons, are around 32 to 33 inches high but about 27 inches wide. Add a few inches above the tank for connections and, on gas, the vent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a short and a lowboy water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A short tank is a standard tank shortened by around 8 to 10 inches, usually still holding 40 gallons, and it suits closets with shelves and low basements. A lowboy is far lower, often under 3 feet tall, and much wider, and it usually holds a few gallons less. Lowboys suit spaces under counters, in crawl spaces and in low attics."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a regular water heater in a mobile home?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Manufactured homes need water heaters listed for that use, and gas units must keep combustion sealed from the living space, usually through a direct vent design. A regular atmospheric tank draws air from the room and is not permitted. Buy a tank labeled for manufactured homes and the vent kit designed for it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 40 gallon gas or electric water heater better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gas recovers about twice as fast, so a 40-gallon gas tank delivers more hot water in a busy hour, and natural gas is cheaper to run in most regions. Electric is simpler to install, needs no vent or combustion air, and fits closets and attics more easily. Usually the fuel your house already has decides it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a 40 gallon water heater last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most tank water heaters last eight to twelve years. A longer warranty, such as the eight years on the GE tanks here, shows the manufacturer's confidence, but maintenance matters more: flush sediment once a year and check the anode rod every three to four years. Hard water and missing expansion tanks on closed systems both shorten tank life."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "40 gallon water heater",
      "40 gallon gas water heater",
      "40 gallon electric water heaters",
      "40 gal water heater",
      "40 gallon hot water heater",
      "lowboy water heater"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">40 Gallon Water Heaters</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Forty gallons is enough for one to three people, if the tank recovers fast enough and fits the space. Here are the tall, short, lowboy and mobile home tanks worth buying, and how to tell when 40 is too small.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ge-gg40t08bxr"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#who-fits" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Who It Fits</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 7 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#first-hour" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. First-Hour Rating</a>
          <a href="#shapes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Tall, Short or Lowboy</a>
          <a href="#closets" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Closets and Attics</a>
          <a href="#mobile-homes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Mobile Homes</a>
          <a href="#too-small" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. When 40 Is Too Small</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Running Cost by Fuel</a>
          <a href="#replacing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Replacing a 40</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Warranty and Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 40 gallon water heater is the right tank for one to three people, and on gas it can serve three with room to spare. What decides whether it works in your home is not the 40 on the label but the first-hour rating, which ranges from about 50 to 83 gallons among the tanks here, and the shape, because a 40-gallon tank can be 5 feet tall, 4 feet tall or knee-high. Choose the first-hour rating for your busiest hour, then the shape that fits the space, then the fuel your house already has.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="who-fits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who a 40-Gallon Water Heater Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 40 gallon water heater is the right tank for one to three people, and in a home on gas it can serve three comfortably. Below 50 gallons the tank is smaller, cheaper and easier to fit, and it loses less heat through its walls every hour of the day. The question is whether it can keep up with your worst hour, and that depends far more on the burner or elements than on the 40 on the label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With the Peak Hour</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the peak. A modern 2-gallon-a-minute showerhead uses about 14 gallons of tank hot water in a ten-minute shower at typical settings. Two showers back to back plus a sink and a short dishwasher cycle is about 40 gallons in an hour. Three showers takes it to about 55. A single person or a couple usually peaks well under 40 gallons, which every tank on this page covers. A household of three is where the choice matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For Three People, the Fuel Decides</h3>

          <p>
