        <div id="first-hour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. First-Hour Rating: Why a Gas 40 Can Beat an Electric 50
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What First-Hour Rating Means</h3>

          <p>
            The first-hour rating, or FHR, is the number of gallons of hot water a tank can deliver in one hour starting full. It appears on the yellow EnergyGuide label and every spec sheet, and it is the number that predicts whether the last shower of the morning runs warm. It combines the usable stored hot water with what the heat source can reheat within the hour, so two tanks of the same size can post very different ratings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas vs Electric Recovery at 40 Gallons</h3>

          <p>
            At 40 gallons, the spread is wide. A 40,000 BTU gas burner reheats around 40 gallons an hour at a 90-degree rise. A 4,500W electric element manages about 20. That is why the GE gas 40 on this page lists 83 gallons in its first hour while 40-gallon electrics sit between about 50 and 63, and why a gas 40 often delivers more in the first hour than a standard electric 50, which lists around 62.
          </p>

