          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Sizing Rule</h3>

          <p>
            The practical rule: count your worst hour, add about 10 percent, and buy a tank whose first-hour rating clears it. If a 40-gallon tank with your fuel cannot reach that number, a larger tank is the answer, not a hotter thermostat. Turning the thermostat up to 130 degrees does stretch a tank by letting you mix in more cold water at the tap, but it raises scald risk and standby loss, so pair it with a thermostatic mixing valve if you go that route.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">On gas, 40 gallons serves three. On electric, it serves two comfortably.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Burner size, not tank size, sets how much hot water you get in a busy hour. If you are on electric with three people who shower back to back, buy the high-recovery short GE or step up to 50 gallons.</p>
          </div>
