          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to choose tall</h3>

          <p>
            Tall tanks fit narrow spaces: between a furnace and a wall, in a slim utility closet, or in a basement corner where floor space is the limit. At 20 inches across, the tall GE, Bradford White and Reliance tanks are the narrowest here. They also tend to be the cheapest, because tall is the default shape.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to choose short</h3>

          <p>
            Short tanks suit closets with a shelf, spaces under stairs and basements with low ducts or beams. The GE short electric and the Rheem short gas both sit around 52 to 53 inches tall, which often clears an obstruction that a 60-inch tank would hit. They are an inch or two wider, so check the door frame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to choose a lowboy</h3>

          <p>
            A lowboy is for spaces under about 4 feet of clear height: under a counter, in a crawl space, or in a low attic. It is wide, so it needs floor space, and its capacity is usually a few gallons less. Choose a lowboy only when height rules out every other shape, and make sure the location still allows access to the elements, anode and drain valve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="closets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Closets, Attics and Tight Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lot of 40-gallon tanks live in small spaces: a hall closet in an apartment, a utility closet in a townhouse, an attic in a slab-on-grade house. The rules for those spaces depend more on fuel than on size. Electric tanks need only a 240V circuit, water lines and access, so they go almost anywhere. Gas tanks need a vent and combustion air, and that limits where they can go.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Tanks in Closets</h3>

          <p>
            For an electric tank in a closet, the main limits are physical. Leave room in front to remove the element covers and reach the drain valve; plumbers need to work on the elements and thermostats eventually. Leave room above to reach the relief valve and water connections. A short tank like the GE40S08DAT is often the right choice because closets tend to have a shelf at around 5 feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Tanks and Combustion Air</h3>

          <p>
            A gas tank in a closet needs a supply of combustion air, usually provided by louvered doors or vents to another room or to the outside, sized by the code your town follows. It also needs its vent connector to rise to the chimney or B-vent with the correct slope and clearances. If those conditions cannot be met, the alternatives are a power vent tank, a direct vent tank, or an electric tank. Our <a href="/best-power-vent-water-heater/">power vent water heater guide</a> covers tanks that vent through a side wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Attics and Replacement Day</h3>

          <p>
            Attics and other spaces above living areas need a drain pan piped to a safe location, because a tank leak there causes expensive damage quickly. Many codes require the pan wherever a leak could damage the building, and also require the relief valve discharge pipe to terminate somewhere visible and safe. If the house has a check valve or pressure-reducing valve on the main, it may need a thermal expansion tank too; our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tank</a> explains when one is required.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the space, think about replacement day. A tank that had to be fitted before the walls went up may not come out through the door. Measure the opening and the route out before you buy, because a 40-gallon tank that has to be cut up to remove adds labor to the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mobile-homes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mobile and Manufactured Homes: Use a Tank Built for Them
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Mobile Homes Need Listed Tanks</h3>

          <p>
            A mobile or manufactured home is not a small house when it comes to water heaters. Federal manufactured-housing standards require water heaters in these homes to be listed for manufactured home use, and gas appliances must be installed so their combustion is separated from the living space. In practice that means a gas tank sold for mobile homes is usually a direct vent or sealed-combustion design, or one installed in a compartment that opens only to the outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            A standard residential atmospheric tank draws combustion air from the room around it and vents into a chimney. In a mobile home's small water heater compartment, that can starve the burner of air or allow exhaust into the home, which is why it is not permitted. The Reliance 6 40 MDV on our list is built for this: it draws air from outside and vents through the same sealed pipe, and it is listed for manufactured homes. Its connections, side inlet and top outlet, also match many mobile home layouts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Options and Other Checks</h3>

          <p>
            Electric water heaters for manufactured homes exist too, and they are labeled for that use; Rheem and others sell versions with an MH suffix. If your home has an electric tank, check the label of the old one and buy a replacement that is also listed for manufactured housing. The circuit, usually 240V and 30 amps, carries over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three more points for mobile homes. First, measure carefully: many mobile home compartments are sized for a specific tank height and diameter, and the Reliance's roughly 58-1/4-inch height and 20-inch diameter are typical. Second, the Reliance's vent kit is sold separately, and you need the one that matches your roof or wall. Third, if your park or lot is on propane, confirm the gas conversion is done correctly before the first light; the Reliance's control is convertible, but conversion should be done by someone qualified. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater guide</a> covers standard gas tanks for site-built homes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-small" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When 40 Gallons Is Too Small
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs the Tank Is Too Small</h3>

          <p>
            A 40-gallon tank is too small when your worst hour regularly beats its first-hour rating. The signs are a lukewarm last shower, a bath that runs cold before it is full, or hot water that cannot cover a shower and the dishwasher together. In a household of four, especially with teenagers, that happens with most 40-gallon electric tanks and many gas ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rule Out Faults First</h3>

          <p>
            Before replacing a tank because it seems small, rule out faults. On an electric tank, a failed lower element is the most common cause of hot water that runs out quickly; the upper element still heats the top of the tank, so you get some hot water, just not much. Our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-element/">water heater element</a> covers how to test and replace one. On any tank, heavy sediment or a broken dip tube can also reduce usable hot water. These repairs cost far less than a new tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stepping Up, or Going Smaller</h3>

          <p>
            If the tank is really too small, the usual answer is 50 gallons. A 50-gallon gas tank lists first-hour ratings in the 80s and above, and a 50-gallon electric with 5,500W elements can reach the mid-70s. If space is the constraint, a gas 40 with a large burner, like the GE RealMAX, can match a 50-gallon electric. Our <a href="/best-50-gallon-water-heater/">50 gallon water heater guide</a> lists tanks with first-hour ratings up to 119 gallons.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also the opposite case. A 40-gallon tank may be more than one person needs, but going smaller rarely saves much. A 30-gallon tank costs only a little less and saves only a little standby loss. Unless you are short on space, a 40 is a sensible minimum for a whole-house tank. For a single bathroom or sink far from the main tank, a point-of-use unit may make more sense; see our guide to the <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point-of-use water heater</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Gas, Electric or Propane: What a 40 Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your Fuel and Our Rate Assumptions</h3>

          <p>
            At 40 gallons, the fuel you already have usually decides the tank. Changing fuel means adding a gas line or a new circuit, which can cost more than the water heater itself. But running costs differ enough that it is worth knowing what you are paying for. Here is a simple estimate for a two to three person household using 45 gallons of hot water a day, heated by 67 degrees. That takes about 25,100 BTU a day, roughly 7.4 kWh of heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            We used $0.17 per kWh for electricity, $1.50 per therm for natural gas, and $2.75 per gallon for propane, close to recent US residential averages. Your rates may be quite different, so substitute your own. The UEF figures are those listed for the tanks on this page.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
