          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Cost Estimates</h3>

          <p>
            These are our calculations from the rated UEF and the assumptions above, not measured bills. They show the general pattern clearly. Natural gas is the cheapest fuel to heat water with in most of the country. Propane costs roughly as much as electricity, sometimes more, depending on your delivery price. Standard electric is the most expensive per gallon in most areas but has the simplest installation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Pump Savings for All-Electric Homes</h3>

          <p>
            For an all-electric home, the big saving is a heat pump water heater, which uses roughly a quarter of the electricity of a standard tank. Most hybrids start at 50 gallons and are tall, so they do not suit the closets and low spaces where 40-gallon tanks often go, but if yours sits in a garage or basement, our guide to the <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">best heat pump water heaters</a> is worth reading before you buy another standard tank. Our <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/">best electric water heater</a> guide covers both standard and hybrid options at every size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Replacing a 40-Gallon Tank: Venting, Wiring and Cost
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Like-for-Like Is Cheapest</h3>

          <p>
            The cheapest replacement keeps everything the same: same fuel, same vent type, similar height and connection positions. A plumber can reuse the vent connector, gas line or electrical connection and the water lines with small changes. Most of the extra cost in a water heater job comes from changing something.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Tanks: Check the Circuit</h3>

          <p>
            For electric tanks, check the circuit. The 4,500W tanks on this page run on a 240V, 30-amp double-pole breaker with 10-gauge copper wire, the standard for residential electric tanks. If the old tank was wired differently, have an electrician check before installation. Also check whether the connections are top or side; the GE lowboy has top ports, and a change in port position adds pipe work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Tanks: Match the Burner and Vent</h3>

          <p>
            For gas tanks, match the burner and the vent. The tanks here range from 32,000 to 40,000 BTU, and a gas line sized for a smaller burner may need to be checked for a larger one. Atmospheric tanks need a vent with proper draft; if the old tank shared a chimney with a furnace that has since been replaced with a high-efficiency unit, the chimney may now be too large for the water heater alone and may need a liner. Your plumber should check this.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost and Professional Installation</h3>

          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost</a> covers typical prices for parts and labor. Many manufacturers require installation by a licensed professional for full warranty coverage, so read the terms before installing it yourself. If you are draining the old tank before the plumber arrives, our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a> explains how to do it safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Warranty and Lifespan: Why Eight Years Stands Out
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Six-Year vs Eight-Year Warranties</h3>

          <p>
            Most 40-gallon tanks carry a six-year limited warranty on the tank and parts. That is what the Rheem, Bradford White and Reliance tanks on this page list. The two GE tanks list eight years, which is a meaningful difference at this price. A longer warranty does not guarantee a longer life, but it does mean the manufacturer covers the most common failure, a leaking tank, for two more years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Warranty Actually Covers</h3>

          <p>
            Read what the warranty actually covers. Tank warranties usually replace a leaking tank, but rarely cover labor after the first year, so a warranty claim usually means a free tank and a paid installation. Parts warranties cover thermostats, elements and gas controls, sometimes for a shorter period than the tank. Registration may be required within a set time after purchase for full coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anode Rods, Sediment and Lifespan</h3>

          <p>
            The life of any tank depends mostly on its anode rod and on sediment. The anode is a metal rod that corrodes in place of the tank steel; once it is used up, the tank starts to rust from the inside. Checking it every three to four years and replacing it when worn can extend a tank's life well beyond the warranty. Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">water heater anode rod replacement</a> explains how. Flushing sediment once a year matters most in hard-water areas, and especially for electric tanks, where sediment buries the lower element.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most tank water heaters last eight to twelve years. If yours is older than that and showing rust or leaks, replace it before it fails; our guides to <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> and <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> help you judge where yours stands.
          </p>
        </div>

