        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many people does a 40 gallon water heater serve?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 40-gallon tank usually serves one to three people. On gas, with a burner around 40,000 BTU, it can serve three comfortably, because first-hour ratings reach the low 80s. On electric, first-hour ratings run about 50 to 63 gallons, so it suits two people comfortably and three with staggered showers. Four or more people should usually buy 50 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 40 gallon water heater big enough for a family of four?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not, especially on electric. A family of four typically uses 55 to 70 gallons in its busiest hour. A gas 40 with a large burner can sometimes keep up if showers are spread out, but most families of four are better served by a 50-gallon gas tank or a 50-gallon electric with high-recovery elements.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tall is a 40 gallon water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tall 40-gallon tanks are about 58 to 62 inches high and around 20 inches across. Short versions are around 52 to 53 inches high and 21 to 22 inches across. Lowboys, which usually hold 36 gallons, are around 32 to 33 inches high but about 27 inches wide. Add a few inches above the tank for connections and, on gas, the vent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a short and a lowboy water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A short tank is a standard tank shortened by around 8 to 10 inches, usually still holding 40 gallons, and it suits closets with shelves and low basements. A lowboy is far lower, often under 3 feet tall, and much wider, and it usually holds a few gallons less. Lowboys suit spaces under counters, in crawl spaces and in low attics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a regular water heater in a mobile home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Manufactured homes need water heaters listed for that use, and gas units must keep combustion sealed from the living space, usually through a direct vent design. A regular atmospheric tank draws air from the room and is not permitted. Buy a tank labeled for manufactured homes and the vent kit designed for it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 40 gallon gas or electric water heater better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas recovers about twice as fast, so a 40-gallon gas tank delivers more hot water in a busy hour, and natural gas is cheaper to run in most regions. Electric is simpler to install, needs no vent or combustion air, and fits closets and attics more easily. Usually the fuel your house already has decides it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 40 gallon water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most tank water heaters last eight to twelve years. A longer warranty, such as the eight years on the GE tanks here, shows the manufacturer's confidence, but maintenance matters more: flush sediment once a year and check the anode rod every three to four years. Hard water and missing expansion tanks on closed systems both shorten tank life.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a standard tank in a mobile home</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufactured homes need a water heater listed for that use, and gas models must keep combustion sealed from the living space. A standard atmospheric tank from a big-box store is the wrong product, even if it fits the compartment. Buy a tank labeled for manufactured homes, such as the Reliance direct vent model, and the matching vent kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every 40 gives the same hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                First-hour ratings for 40-gallon tanks run from about 50 to 83 gallons. An electric 40 that ran out of hot water will be replaced by another electric 40 that runs out the same way. If the old tank could not keep up, buy more recovery, such as gas or a high-recovery electric, or move to 50 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting that lowboys hold less</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most tanks sold as 40-gallon lowboys actually hold about 36 gallons, and they are much wider than a tall tank. Check the actual capacity, width and first-hour rating before choosing one, and use a lowboy only when the space is too low for a short tank, not just because it looks compact.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring combustion air in a closet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gas tank in a closet needs combustion air through louvered doors or vents, sized by code. Without it, the burner can produce carbon monoxide and the vent may spill. If the closet cannot provide enough air, choose an electric tank or a sealed direct vent model instead, and fit a carbon monoxide alarm nearby either way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the wrong gas type</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tank built for natural gas cannot burn propane without a conversion, and most residential tanks are not designed for field conversion. If your home runs on propane, buy a model built for LP, like the A.O. Smith GCR-40LP, or a convertible one listed for it, and check the gas type on the label before installation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

